							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left">It&#039;s a bit encounter dependant really. In EQ especially the base strat is usually trivial as hell.<br /><br />I mean you come to, say, Emp Ssra room and you see some adds with the Emp. The massive strat variety available:<br />1. Kill Emperor while CCing the adds that can be CCd and offtanking the ones that can&#039;t.<br />2. Kill some of the adds, then CC/tank the rest while you kill the Emperor.<br />3. Kill all the adds, then kill the Emperor.    (usually the best, unless there&#039;s some hardcoded surprise in there, which is quite common)<br /><br />I mean that base level of strategy sure took a long fucking time to figure out after porting up there. After that you have a few things you need to know:<br />X = the amount of damage your healers can heal without losing mana<br />Y = dps from average add<br />Z = dps from named<br />D = collective manapool<br /><br />If  Z + n*Y &gt; X, you are probably going to lose (or did during Luclin and PoP anyway). Then you have to make sure you reduce &#039;n&#039; to controllable levels ASAP. That means strat #2. If the adds can all be killed, they probably should be.<br /><br />Then there&#039;s the damage your raid can dish out etc.<br /><br />The trick is usually optimizing the damage your raid does and X while minimizing Y and Z. Y and Z were minimized by figuring out the AE, where it was (PBAE, targeted or from an invisible NPC) and what it&#039;s range was (lucy). Combine this information to make a quick map of the area and to find out safe spots. If none are to be found, just go with the &quot;healers under fire&quot; scenario, that was well enough rehearsed.<br /><br />Either way I would probably be able to write a single (rather goddamn long) formula for beating EQ raid encounters that would probably still be perfectly valid.<br /><br /><br />And like Shrek said, the challenge was increased constantly by requiring the core that can&#039;t fuck up being larger. On the other hand they kept adding to the organizational challenge by making raids bigger and bigger. This sorta maxed out at Rathe when they realized it was getting retarded. Then they went smaller and just tried increasing the &quot;core&quot; to as close to the full raid as was possible without making the class requirements insane. Here&#039;s IMO where EQ shot itself in the foot by introducing so many classes. WoW does this a great deal better mostly due to the fact that there&#039;s only 8 classes, making balancing encounters that much easier. </td>
						</tr>
